1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,632
This enzyme has a name.

2
00:00:02,682 --> 00:00:10,031
TEM. After Temoneira, a patient in Athens, 1963, whose E. coli ate penicillin.

3
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:11,548
Where did it come from?

4
00:00:11,598 --> 00:00:12,694
Not from the chromosome.

5
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:13,828
From this.

6
00:00:13,878 --> 00:00:18,308
A plasmid: a spare ring of DNA with one gene on it.

7
00:00:18,358 --> 00:00:19,362
A passenger.

8
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:26,357
The gene is read, the protein is made, and it is shipped across the inner membrane to wait by the door.

9
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,210
Now the part that should worry you.

10
00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:30,372
Bacteria have sex.

11
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,795
A pilus hooks a neighbour that has no enzyme.

12
00:00:33,845 --> 00:00:35,754
One strand of the ring crosses.

13
00:00:35,804 --> 00:00:37,461
The neighbour completes it.

14
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:40,516
It did not evolve resistance.

15
00:00:40,566 --> 00:00:41,727
It was handed it.

16
00:00:41,777 --> 00:00:42,511
In minutes.

17
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:47,378
Same drug.

18
00:00:47,428 --> 00:00:48,305
Same door.

19
00:00:48,355 --> 00:00:49,419
Ring broken.

20
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:55,519
Resistance does not need a stronger cell.

21
00:00:55,569 --> 00:00:57,690
It needs a gene that travels.

